    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d653">

                <pb facs="0687" n="675" sameAs="frus:frus1941v02/pg675"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">868.248/118</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Chief of the Division of Near Eastern
                        Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Murray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Assistant Secretary
                        of State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Berle</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-01-14">January 14, 1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Berle</hi>: I have read with interest the
                    memorandum of your conversation with the British Chargé d’Affaires on January 10
                    regarding planes for Greece.</p>
                <p>With reference to the final Note, I may say that in a telephone conversation with
                    Mr. Butler this morning he watered down considerably the picture he had
                    previously given us as to the immediate availability of the Mohawk planes. He
                    now says that he may have been too optimistic in that regard since some of the
                    planes in question did not leave this country until about December 25 and that
                    they are proceeding to Egypt by way of Capetown. It would thus appear that the
                    planes which we had been given to believe were in Egypt or would arrive there
                    imminently and could be flown without delay to Greece are not quite so near
                    their goal.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wallace Murray</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
